Script: 
Characters: Rohan (host), Aarav (Indian zodiac fan), Wei (Chinese zodiac fan), Kabir (skeptic), Pandit Rao (Vedic astrologer), Master Lin (Chinese astrologer)
Setting: A college fest stall called "What's Your Sign?"
Total: 6 scenes, about 1 minute each
Scene 1: The Argument (1 min)
ROHAN: (to audience) Welcome to our stall! Today's question: whose zodiac is better, Indian or Chinese?
AARAV: Indian, obviously. I'm a Simha, a Leo, and proud of it.
WEI: Please, bro. I'm a Dragon. Powerful, lucky, legendary.
KABIR: Or you're both just reading horoscopes at a college fest.
ROHAN: Kabir, not helping. Let's call the experts. (claps) Pandit Rao! Master Lin!
(Both enter from opposite sides and bow.)
Scene 2: The Chinese Zodiac (1 min)
MASTER LIN: In Chinese astrology, your sign depends on your birth year. There are 12 animals: Rat, Ox, Tiger, Rabbit, Dragon, Snake, Horse, Goat, Monkey, Rooster, Dog, Pig.
KABIR: Why those animals?
MASTER LIN: A legend. The Jade Emperor held a river race, and the first 12 animals to finish got a year named after them.
WEI: The Rat won by riding on the Ox, then jumping off right at the finish line!
AARAV: That's cheating!
MASTER LIN: That's clever. The cycle repeats every 12 years, and five elements, Wood, Fire, Earth, Metal and Water, add more layers.
Scene 3: The Indian Zodiac (1 min)
PANDIT RAO: In Vedic astrology, your sign depends on the position of the Moon at your birth. That is your Chandra Rashi.
AARAV: We have 12 Rashis too: Mesha, Vrishabha, Mithuna, Karka, Simha, and more.
KABIR: So 12 and 12. Same thing?
PANDIT RAO: Not quite. We also have 27 Nakshatras, nine planets called the Navagraha, and a full birth chart, the Kundli. We use the sidereal zodiac, based on the stars' actual positions, so many people's signs shift by one from the Western system.
AARAV: Wait. I might not be a Leo?
PANDIT RAO: (smiling) Possibly a Cancer.
(Aarav gasps dramatically.)
Scene 4: The Clash (1 min)
ROHAN: So Chinese astrology is about time, your birth year. Indian astrology is about sky position, your Moon and planets.
WEI: Mine is simple. One animal, one year.
AARAV: Mine is detailed. Moon, planets, houses, everything.
KABIR: But both predict personality and compatibility.
ROHAN: Right! India has Kundli Milan before weddings. China checks animal compatibility, like Dragon with Rooster.
WEI: And some pairs are bad luck. Tiger and Monkey, for example.
AARAV: We have Manglik Dosha. Half of India panics over it.
(Everyone laughs.)
Scene 5: The Twist (1 min)
KABIR: So who's right?
MASTER LIN: Neither claims to be science. Both are ways of finding patterns in life.
PANDIT RAO: Both come from thousands of years of people watching time, the sky, and each other.
ROHAN: And both shape festivals. Diwali follows the lunar calendar, and Chinese New Year follows the animal cycle.
WEI: So we're more alike than we thought.
AARAV: Fine. Chinese wins on cool animals. (beat) Indian wins on drama.
KABIR: I'm still not convinced. But the Rat-on-the-Ox story is great.
Scene 6: Closing (1 min)
ROHAN: (to audience) So, your turn. Are you a Dragon or a Simha?
WEI: A Rat or a Cancer?
AARAV: Or still confused like me?
KABIR: (sighs) Even I'll admit it's fun.
MASTER LIN: Different roads...
PANDIT RAO: ...same curiosity about the stars, and ourselves.
ALL: (together) The stars, and the stories, are yours!
(All bow. End.)

